== The First Sermon

#quote[On this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make for all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined.—Isa 25:6.]

In the previous chapter the holy prophet had spoken of the miseries and desolation of the church in many heavy, sad, and doleful expressions, such as “the vine withers; the earth is defiled by its people, because they have transgressed the laws, overstepped the decrees, and broken the everlasting covenant; therefore a curse has consumed the earth, and those who dwell in it no longer drink wine with a song.” Here you#super[+] see that all sweetness and rejoicing of heart has departed from them. Yet even in the midst of all these miseries God, the God of comforts, makes sweet and gracious promises to his church, to raise it out of its mournful state and condition. And therefore the prophet, in the first part of this chapter, speaks of blessing God for the destruction of his enemies and for his great love for his church. And when he had spoken of the ruin of the enemy, he at once breaks out with thanksgiving, breathing out abundant praises to his God. For it is the custom of holy men, guided by the prompting of the blessed Spirit of God, to praise his name on all occasions, but especially for benefits to his church: not out of ill will at the destruction of the adversaries, but at the execution of divine justice, for the fulfilling of the truth of his promise; as in the first verse of this chapter: “O #smallcaps[Lord], You are my God! I will exalt You; I will praise Your name. For You have worked wonders—plans formed long ago—in faithfulness and truth.” When the things that were promised of old were brought to pass, the church was always ready to give God the glory of his truth. Therefore, do not rejoice when your enemies fall; but when the enemies of the Lord are brought to desolation, then we may, indeed we ought to, sing “Hallelujah” to him who lives forever and ever.

I will now come to the very words of my text: “On this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make for all people a feast of fat things.” These words are prophetic, and cannot be perfectly fulfilled all at once, but they will be fulfilled by degrees. The promise of “a new heaven and a new earth” (2 Pet 3:13) will be fulfilled. The conversion of the Jews, and the bringing in of the fullness of the Gentiles, will be brought to pass by degrees. All the promises that God has ever made will be accomplished before the second coming of Christ to judgment. God has made his peace with us in the gospel of peace; and when all these promises are fulfilled, then all imperfection will be done away, and we will never be removed from our Rock, but our joy will then be full. Indeed, even in this life we have some degrees of perfection. We have grace, and the means of grace, the ordinances of Christ, and a testimony of everlasting glory. “On this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make a feast.”

In these words you#super[+] have a glorious and royal feast set down. The place where this feast is to be kept is “Mount Zion”; the feast-maker is “the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts”; the parties invited are “all people”; what comes of it, and the provision for the feast, are “fat things” and “wine” of the best: a feast of the best of the best, a feast of the fat and of the marrow, a feast of “wine on the lees well refined.”

Here you#super[+] may see that God veils heavenly things under earthly things, and stoops so low that he enters the inner man by way of the outward man. For our powers of understanding are so weak and narrow that we cannot become acquainted with spiritual things except by the inward working of the Spirit of the Almighty.

This “mountain” is the place where this feast is made, namely “Mount Zion,” which is a type and figure of the church, called in Scripture “the holy mountain.” For as mountains are raised high above the earth, so the church of God is raised in excellence and dignity above all sorts of mankind.

Observation 1. The church is raised as far above all men as men are above beasts. This mountain is above all mountains. “The mountain of the #smallcaps[Lord]” is above every other mountain. “You, O mountain, will stand immovable,” when all other mountains will smoke if they are only touched. This is the mountain of mountains. The church of God is most excellent in glory and dignity, as you#super[+] may see at the end of the previous chapter, where the glory of the church outshines every other glory: “The moon,” says the prophet, “will be confounded and the sun will be ashamed; for the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will reign on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem, and before His elders with great glory.” So the brightness of the church will outshine the glory of the sun and of the moon. Thus you#super[+] see that the church of God is a mountain.

Reason. First, because God has established it on a stronger foundation than all the rest of the world. It is founded on the goodness and power and truth of God. Mountains of bronze and iron are not so firm as this mountain. For what sustains the church but the word of God? And being built on his word and truth, it may very well be called a mountain, for it will be like Mount Zion, which will never be removed (Ps 125:1). It may be moved, but never removed. So, because of its firmness and stability, it may rightly be called a mountain.

Observation 2. Again, we may here speak in some sort of the visibility of the church. But here the papists will raise a quarrel. When they hear of this mount, they at once apply it to their church. Their church, they say, is a mount; so says the Scripture.

I answer, first, we grant that in some sort their church is a mount (though not this mount), for Babylon is built on seven hills; but if this proves her a church, it is an antichristian church. Secondly, the universal Protestant church has always had a being, though at times invisible. The apostle, writing to the Romans, exhorts them “not to be high-minded, but to fear”; for, he says, “if God has broken off the natural branches, perhaps he will break you#super[+] off also” (Rom 11:20, 21). And indeed, for their pride and haughtiness of mind, they are broken off at this day. Christ, who “walks among the seven golden lampstands” (Rev 2:1), never said that the church of Smyrna or Ephesus would always remain a visible church to the eyes of the world, and they did not; for to this very day they lie under bondage and slavery to the Turks. The mount has always been visible, though not always equally gloriously visible. For there will be a time when the church will flee into the wilderness (Rev 12:6). Where, then, will the glorious visibility of the church be? There is a time when all will follow the beast. The papists themselves confess that in Antichrist’s time the church will scarcely be visible. The essence of a thing and the quality of a thing may differ. The church is a church, and visible, but not always equally and alike gloriously visible. Yet those who had spiritual eyes, and looked on things through the spectacles of the Scripture, could always declare that the church was visible. For from the beginning of the world the church has always had luster enough to delight and draw the elect, and so it will have to the end of the world, though at times the church may have a mist before it, as Augustine says: “It is no wonder that you cannot see a mountain, for you have no eyes.” But the papists have seen this mountain. As they have always been bloody persecutors of the church, they have seen enough to put them to shame. For we have nothing in our church that they do not have as well; only ours is refined and freed from idolatry. We have two sacraments, they have seven. We have Scripture, they have traditions, which they make equal with it. We have the Scriptures pure, they corrupt. So our church was in the midst of theirs, as a sound and purer part in a corrupt body.

This mountain is the church. “The Lamb” is “standing on Mount Zion, and with Him 144,000 who had … His Father’s name written on their foreheads” (Rev 14:1). Christ stands in the church, and standing on Mount Zion he is accompanied by those whom his Father gave him before the world was. Therefore those who belong to this holy mountain are Christ’s. “And on this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make for all people a feast.” And this feast is a royal feast, a marriage feast, in which the joy and comfort of God’s people are set out by what is most comforting among men. The founder of the feast is “the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts.” It is only he who is able to prepare a table in the wilderness, who is mighty and able to feast his church with a spiritual and holy banquet. We all live at his table for the feeding of our bodies, but much more for our souls. He can make a feast for the whole man, for he is Lord of the conscience; and he is the one to spread a table for the whole world. Indeed, more: if there were so many, he could furnish a table for ten thousand worlds. He is the God of all spiritual comforts, and “the God of all comfort.” He is infinite and can never be drawn dry, for he is the fountain of eternal life. All graces and comforts in Scripture are called the comforts and graces of the Holy Spirit, because God is the giver of them by his Spirit. Who can take away the wound of a guilty conscience but he who has set the conscience in the hearts of men? If he pleases, he can take away the burden of a grieved conscience and give it new and solid comforts in its place. He knows all the windings and turnings of the soul, where all the pain and grief lies; and he cannot fail to know it, because he alone is above the soul. He is therefore the fittest to make the soul a feast. He alone can do it, and he will do it.

#quote[“On this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make a feast.” Why is he called “the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts”?]

It is a usual term to set forth the glory of God, to make his power and the greatness of his majesty known among the children of men.

#quote[“He will make for all people a feast.”]

Those who are invited to this glorious feast are “all people.” None excepted, none excluded, who will come in to Christ! Some of all sorts, of all nations, of all languages! This relates to the time of the gospel. The church at first had its being in particular families, but afterward was more enlarged. The church at the first was of the daughters of men and the sons of God. The children of the church mingled with a generation of corrupt persons who would keep within no bounds; but after Abraham’s time there was another generation of the church, so that it was a little more enlarged. Then there was a third generation, a divided generation, made up of Jews and Gentiles. So when Christ came into the world, the bounds of the church began to enlarge more and more, so that now it is in this happy condition: “Come to Me, all you#super[+] who are weary and burdened” (Matt 11:28). Both Jews and Gentiles, all are invited, whoever they are; “nothing is now unclean” (Acts 10:15). Christ has come, and has made “for all people a feast of fat things.” It must be a feast, and of fat things, for all the world will be the better for it. The Jews will be converted, and the fullness of the Gentiles will come in. And yet it is no harm to any particular man, because the things you#super[+] are to taste are spiritual. Go to all the good things in the world: the more one has of them, the less another must have, because they are earthly, and so finite. But in spiritual things all may have the whole, and every man in particular. Every man enjoys the light of the sun in particular, and all enjoy it too. So the whole church, and only the church, enjoys the benefit and comfort of this feast; but under the name of this church come all the elect, both Jews and Gentiles. And therefore it must be the Lord of Hosts who can make such a feast as this, a feast for all people. No other is able to do it.

This feast is “a feast of fat things, full of marrow, and of wine on the lees well refined,” the best that can be imagined, the best of the best. A feast is promised, a spiritual feast. The special graces and favors of God are compared to a feast made up of the best things, full of all varieties and excellences; and the chief dish, which is all in all, is Christ, and all the gracious benefits we can in any way expect from him by promise. All other favors and blessings, whatever they are, are only Christ dished out, if I may put it that way, in various offices and attributes. He is the source of comfort, the principle of grace and holiness. All is included in Christ. Ask him and you#super[+] will receive, even the forgiveness of your#super[+] sins, peace of conscience, and the communion of saints. Ask Christ, as one invested with all privileges for the good of others. But this is through his death. He is the feast itself. He is dished out into promises. Do you#super[+] have a promise of the pardon of sins? It is from Christ.

Would you have peace of conscience? It is from Christ. Justification and redemption? It is from Christ. The love of God is conveyed to us by Christ; indeed, all the good that we have is only Christ parceled out.

#quote[Now I will show why Christ, with his benefits, prerogatives, graces, and comforts, is compared to a feast.]

First. In respect of the choiceness of the things. In a feast all things are of the best; so are the things we have in Christ. Whatever favors we have by Christ, they are choice ones. They are the best of everything. Pardon for sin is a pardon of pardon. The title we have to heaven through him is a sure title. The joy we have by him is the joy of all joys. The liberty and freedom from sin that he purchased for us by his death is perfect freedom. The riches of grace we have by him are the only lasting and durable riches. Take anything you#super[+] can: if we have it by Christ, it is of the best. All worldly excellences and honors are only shadows compared with the high excellences and honor we have in Christ. No joy, no comfort, no peace, no riches, no inheritance can be compared with the joy, peace, and inheritance that we have in Christ. Whatever we have by him, we have it in a glorious manner. And therefore he is compared to fat, to “fat things full of marrow,” to “wine,” to “wine on the lees,” which preserves its freshness; the best wine of all, which is not changed from vessel to vessel, but keeps its strength. And indeed, the strength and vigor of all flows from Jesus Christ in covenant with us.

#quote[The love of Christ is the best love, and he himself is incomparably the best, and has favors and blessings of the choicest kind.]

Second. Again, as in a feast there is variety besides choiceness, so in Christ there is variety to answer all our needs. Are we foolish? He is wisdom. Do we have guilt in our consciences? He is righteousness, and this righteousness is imputed to us. Are we defiled? He is sanctification. Are we in misery? He is our redemption. If there are a thousand kinds of evils in us, there are a thousand ways to remedy them by Jesus Christ. Therefore the good things we have by Christ are compared to all the benefits we have in this world. In Christ is choice and variety. Are we weak? He is food to feed us, so that we may be strong. He will refresh us. He is the best of foods. He is marrow. So, are our spirits faint? He is wine. Thus we have in Christ what supplies all our needs. He is variety.

There is a plant among the Indians called coquus \[the coco palm\]; its fruit serves for food and drink, to comfort and refresh the body. It yields that from which the people make clothing to clothe themselves, and also what is medicinal, very good against the disorders of the body. And if God will infuse so much power into a poor plant, what power may we expect to be in Christ himself? He feeds our souls to all eternity, puts on us the robes of righteousness, and heals the disorders of our souls. There is variety in him for all our needs whatever. He is food, medicine, and clothing to clothe us; and when we are clothed with him, we may stand with boldness before the majesty of God. He is all in all. He is variety, and all. There is something in Christ to answer all the necessities of God’s people, and not only so, but to their full contentment in everything.

Third. Again, as there is variety in a feast, so there is sufficiency, full sufficiency. “We have seen … the one and only Son from the Father, full of grace and truth” (John 1:14). And being full of grace, he is wise and able to furnish this heavenly banquet with enough of all sorts of provisions fit for the soul to feed on. There is abundance of grace, and excellence, and sufficiency in Christ. And it must be so, because he is a Savior of God’s own sending. “Do not work for food that perishes, but for the food … which the Son of God will give you#super[+]. For on Him God the Father has placed His seal” (John 6:27); that is, he is sent out for this purpose, to “feed the church of God” (Acts 20:28). As there is an all-sufficiency in God, so there is in Christ, who by the sacrifice of himself was able to give satisfaction to divine justice. Therefore he says, “My flesh is real food, and My blood is real drink” (John 6:55); that is, spiritually, to the soul, he is real food, and can satisfy God’s justice. If we consider him as God alone, he is “a consuming fire” (Heb 12:29); or as man alone, he can do nothing; but considered as God-man, he is real food and real drink. And now the soul is content with what divine justice is content with. Though our consciences are large, yet God is larger and above our consciences. Therefore, as there is variety of excellence, so there is sufficiency and fullness in Christ. What he did, he did to the full. He is a Savior, and he fills up that name to the full. His pardon for sin is a full pardon; his merits for us are full merits; his satisfaction to divine justice is a full satisfaction; his redemption of our souls and bodies is a full redemption. Thus all he did was full.

Fourth. A feast is for company. It is convivium \[a living together\]. There is conversation at it. So Cicero prefers the Latin name convivium to the Greek name συμπόσιον \[a drinking together\]. And this feast is not for one. We are all invited to it. The excellence of Christ’s feast consists in the communion of saints; for the spirits of all who take part in it must agree with one another. Love is the best and chief dish at this feast. The more we partake of the sweetness of Christ, the more we love one another. Christ by his Spirit so works in the hearts of the children of men that, if a thousand are brought together from a thousand different nations, within a little while you#super[+] will have them all acquainted with one another. If they are good, there is agreement of spirit, and a sympathy between them. There is a kinship in Christ. He is the true Isaac. The death of Christ and the blood of Christ are the ground of all union and joy and comfort of every kind. The blood of Christ sprinkled on the conscience will bring that peace of conscience which will be a continual feast to the soul. This feast must be wonderfully comforting, for we do not feast with those who are like ourselves, but we feast with God the Father, and the Holy Spirit, sent by Christ, procured by the death of Christ. The angels attend us at this feast; therefore it must be joyful. No joy is comparable to the joy of a feast. This is not just any feast. This is a marriage feast, at which we are betrothed to Christ. Now of all feasts, marriage feasts are the most sumptuous. This is a marriage feast for the King’s Son, for Christ himself; and therefore of necessity it must be full of all choice varieties, of the sweetest things, of the most excellent things, and of the quintessence of things. Here is all the joy that belongs to a feast. Here it is, to be had with Christ. What acquaintance can be more glorious than the one between Jesus Christ and a Christian soul? When we have hope of better things to come, then we find the sweetness of this communion. No harmony in the world can be so sweet as the harmony kept between Christ and the soul. When we have this, and are made one with God in Christ, our joy must then be inexpressible. Once the contract is made between the soul and Christ, there cannot fail to be abundant joy. When the soul is joined with Christ by faith, it cannot help finding its solace in a perpetual jubilee and a perpetual feast, in some degree.

Fifth. Again, for a feast you#super[+] have the choicest garments, as at the marriage of the Lamb, “fine linen, bright and pure” (Rev 19:8), which is the righteousness of the saints. When God sees these robes on us and the Spirit of Christ in us, then there is a robe of righteousness imputed, and a garment of holiness, with which our souls are clothed. So this is a feast that must have wonderfully glorious attire; and when this marriage is consummated, we are sure to have a garment of glory put on us.

Sixth. This was signified in old times among the Jews.

+ In the feast of the Passover (not to name all the resemblances, but only one or two). The lamb for the Passover, you#super[+] know, was chosen out of the flock from among the rest four days before the time appointed for that feast. So Christ is the true Paschal Lamb, chosen by God before the foundation of the world was laid, to be slain for us.
+ Again, manna was a type of Christ. It came from heaven to feed the hungry bodies of the Israelites in the wilderness. In the same way Christ came, sent from God the Father, to be the eternal food and upholder of the souls and bodies of every one of us. Manna was white and sweet; so is Christ, white in righteousness and holiness, and also sweet to delight the soul. Manna fell on the tents in the night; and Christ came when darkness was spread over all the world. God gave manna freely from heaven; so Christ was a free gift, and he freely gave himself to death, even to the cursed death of the cross, for us. All, both poor and rich, gathered manna. Christ is a common food for king and subject. All share in Christ. Neither Jew nor Gentile is shut out, but all may come and buy freely without money. Of this manna, he who had least had enough. So here, he who has least of Christ will still have enough, though he takes him with a trembling hand, for Christ is his. Whoever has the least grace, if it is true and sound, has grace enough to bring him to eternal life. The Jews wondered at the manna, saying, “What is it?” So one of Christ’s names is “Wonderful” (Isa 9:6). Grace and favor from Christ is true spiritual manna to the soul. Manna fell in the wilderness: even so we must remain in the wilderness of this wretched world until we come to heaven. Christ is manna to us, and very sweet as he is conveyed to us in his word and sacraments. When the Israelites came into the land of Canaan the manna ceased, not before. So when we come to heaven, the elect’s purchased possession, we will have another kind of manna for our souls. We will not feed on Christ there as in the sacrament; no, we will see him “face to face, and know as we are known” (1 Cor 13:12). In the wilderness of this world it is fitting for God to convey this heavenly manna to the soul in whatever way he pleases. Manna could not fall until the Israelites had used up all the provision they brought with them out of Egypt; and we cannot taste that heavenly manna of our Father until our souls are drawn away from all dependence on the world and from carnal delights.

  Then, indeed, manna will be sweet and precious. What is this heavenly manna, what are Christ and his Father, what are the word and sacraments, to a depraved, vicious heart, stuffed full of earthly vanities? Alas! it loathes all these. As none tasted manna but those who came out of Egypt, so none will taste Christ but those who are not of the world, who have come out of Egypt, out of sin and darkness. Manna fell only around the tents of Israel, and in no other part of the world but there, so that none might have the privilege of eating it but God’s own special, chosen people. Christ falls on the tents of the righteous, and none will taste this blessed spiritual food but those who are the Israel of God, who are of the church, who feel the burden of sin and groan under it. Oh! the very taste of this heavenly manna is sweet to their souls, and to none but them. Thus you#super[+] see the feast that Christ makes for us on Mount Zion, and that this manna typifies Christ with all his benefits.

+ Again, the hard rock in the wilderness, when it was struck with the rod of Moses, at once gushed out water in abundance, which preserved the life of the Israelites. So Christ, the rock of our salvation, the strength of his church, the rock and fortress of all his saints: when his precious side was gored with the bloody lance on the cross, the blood gushed out in such a manner and such abundance that by its shedding our souls are preserved alive. He is both manna and the rock of water. Manna had all in it, so had the rock; and all necessities are plentifully supplied by Christ. The church of God has always had bread to satisfy spiritual hunger. It never lacked necessary comforts. It is said (Rev 12:6), “When the church fled into the wilderness, God fed her there,” alluding to the children of Israel fed by manna. The Jews did not lack in the wilderness, nor has the church of God ever lacked comfort, even in the midst of the persecution and oppression of all her enemies. When Elijah was in the wilderness, he was fed (1 Kgs 17:4, 6). The church of God will be fed not only in her body but in her soul, for Christ has hidden manna for his elect. This typifies the exceeding joy of the church, the hidden manna, “what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no heart has imagined” (1 Cor 2:9); no heart of man can conceive of those joys that the church of God will have when the marriage is consummated. Joy in the Holy Spirit and peace of conscience are hidden from the world, and sometimes from God’s people themselves, though they will enjoy them hereafter.
+ All the former feasts in times past were only types of this. The Feast of Tabernacles, the feast of the Passover, the spiritual manna, and all other holy feasts were only to signify and show forth this feast by Christ. But there is this difference between the type and the thing signified. In the type, the Passover lamb was quite eaten up; but this Passover, Christ, who was slain for sin, can never be eaten up. We feed on him with our souls. He cannot be consumed like the Passover lamb, or like manna, which was gone when the sun rose. Indeed, the manna that was laid up for a remembrance before the ark came to nothing, but Christ is in heaven forevermore for the soul to feed on. Though these were resemblances, yet they failed, as it is fitting that resemblances should fail, that is, come short of the body of the thing itself. Thus you#super[+] see that the spiritual comforts of a Christian may well and fitly be compared to a feast.
+ Thus you#super[+] see that God provides a feast and invites all. In the sacrament you#super[+] have a feast, a feast of varieties, not only bread, but wine—to show the variety and fullness of comfort in Christ. He intends full comfort. As for our adversaries the papists, they have dry feasts. They give the people the bread, but the wine they keep for themselves. But God in Christ intends full comfort for us. Whatever Christ did, it was full. His merits are complete, and his joy was full. He is fullness itself; and therefore whatever comes from him must be, as he himself is, both full and sweet. He intends full consolation for us.

Use. Therefore we ought to be prepared to partake of this feast in such a way that we may have full joy and full comfort; for there is enough in Christ to satisfy all the hungry souls in the world, and he himself is present at this heavenly banquet. All fullness dwells in him (Col 1:19), from which “we have all received grace upon grace.” Therefore,

Use 1. Let us labor to have large hearts: for as our faith grows more and more, so we will carry away more comfort and more strength from this holy feast. As with the poor widow: if her vessels had not failed, the oil would not have ceased; if there had been more vessels, there would have been more oil (2 Kgs 4:6). Our souls are like these vessels. Let us therefore labor, and make it our great business, to have large souls, souls able to drink in this spiritual oil of gladness; for we will carry as much comfort away from Christ as we bring faith to him. Because the favors of God in Christ are infinite, the more we draw from him, the more glory we give him. But if they were finite, we would offend his bounty; he might soon be drawn dry, and so send us away with a comfortless answer, that he was not able to relieve us. But Christ is infinite, and the more we have from him, the more we may have. “Whoever has will be given more” (Matt 13:12). The more often we go to Christ, the more honor and glory we bring him. This is a banquet to the full.

We have now come to the banquet, and Christ is the founder of it; indeed, he is the feast itself. He is the author of it, and he it is on whom we feed.

Use 2. Let us labor not to be narrow receivers of the sacrament, but to suck in abundance from Christ with a great deal of delight, so that we may come together not for the worse but for the better, considering how great a measure of strength and grace is required as very necessary for maintaining spiritual life.
